{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8dd772eb105a56f8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8002\n3\tscore: 13\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n9\t  description: E006 repo_access is +1 while the comparable non-code deliverables E005\n10\t    and E007 score 0; a content-authoring epic is not repo-based work in the same\n11\t    sense — defensible via the in-repo override lookup structure, but inconsistent\n12\t    across sibling deliverable epics.\n13\t- severity: minor\n14\t  criterion: Completeness\n15\t  description: Strategy success criterion Scalability (handles large numbers of queued\n16\t    workloads) is not an explicit acceptance criterion on any epic; only the E002\n17\t    UI-responsiveness performance AC partially covers it.\n18\terror: null\n19\t---\n20\t## Review Summary\n21\t\n22\tScore: 13/14 — pass\n23\tRecommendation: accept\n24\t\n25\tSeven epics, no conditional branches. Triage is null (full decomposition), so the multi-epic output is expected and the Step 1.5 triage contradiction check does not apply. The decomposition is structurally sound: DAG edges match frontmatter in both directions, all 11 HLRs are traceable, work-product types are correctly separated per team, and both strategy open questions are legitimately non-gating. One arguable AI-signal value costs a point; no critical or major issues found.\n26\t\n27\t## Criterion Details\n28\t\n29\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n30\t\n31\tAll 11 HLRs (5 P0, 4 P1, 2 P2) map to at least one epic; the traceability matrix in the decomposition summary is present and matches each epic's \"HLR Traceability\" section. Priority inheritance is correct: E001 (P0) is the only prerequisite of P1 epics (P0 prerequisite of P1 dependents is fine). E005/E006 refine P0 HLRs at P1 but are not DAG prerequisites of any P0 epic — the placeholder-and-replace pattern is explicitly mandated by the strategy, so this is not a priority inversion. E007 is `docs-authoring` and is exempt from priority inheritance; its P1 correctly derives from the strategy's Major priority. Note (not scored): E001 bundles the two P2 HLRs (structured status derivation model; WaitForRecovery/WaitForStart and AdmissionCheck granularity) with P0 work. Both P2 items are polish on the same derivation/lookup code the P0 rework already touches — they are not independently deferrable features, so the bundling is acceptable.\n32\t\n33\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n34\t\n35\tNo circular dependencies. Cross-checked every diagram edge against epic frontmatter `dependencies` in both directions: E001→E002, E001→E006, and E001–E006→E007 all match exactly, with no extra frontmatter dependencies and no references to nonexistent epics. Arrow direction follows the dependency→dependent convention. Edge justification is sound: E001→E002 (Rule 12, framework→consumer — position display renders into the status surface and needs pending-state determination), E001→E006 (Rule 13, artifact producer→consumer — the override lookup structure and enumerated condition/reason set), and all six edges into E007 (Rule 11, docs-authoring always last; explicitly not a serialization or priority-inheritance concern). No konflux-onboarding or other implementation-type coupling exists in this decomposition. Parallelism is handled correctly: E001/E003/E004 are parallel per Rule 15 (same repo, different areas, no shared build-against artifact), and E005's lack of edges to E003/E004 is the strategy's explicit \"development must not be blocked on UX\" mandate, documented in the DAG justification. Critical path length 3 (E001→E006→E007) is one above the S-size heuristic (1–2), but it is forced by Rule 11 (docs last) plus the Rule 13 content edge — acceptable, not scored against. Scanned epic content for uncaptured data dependencies: E004's LocalQueue-list resolution and E003's `buildWorkloadMapForNotebooks()` reuse existing platform data; E005's \"specified against the implemented conditional behaviors\" refers to strategy-defined behaviors, not artifacts produced by E003/E004 — no missing edges.\n36\t\n37\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n38\t\n39\tEach component/team tuple produces separate epics: (AI Core Dashboard, Dashboard UI) is split into four epics by work area (status model, Visibility API, indicators, HP forms), plus (UXD, UXD), (AI Core Dashboard, Content), and (Documentation, Docs). No epic spans multiple teams or components. No epic is oversized — E001 is the largest but is bounded to a single frontend status-model rework well within a sprint or two. No scope duplication: E001's engineering-default copy vs E006's final wording, and E003/E004's placeholder treatments vs E005's finalized designs, are distinct work products in the strategy's mandated placeholder-and-replace pattern, not independent builds of the same artifact. No work-product type mixing — code, design specs, content, and documentation are each in separate epics even where teams could have been combined. Minor observation (not scored): the joint UXD/Content review of status-message wording appears in both E005 and E006 scope; acceptable because it is a single shared review activity with distinct deliverables on each side.\n40\t\n41\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n42\t\n43\tAll seven epics are typed Implementation, and all genuinely produce concrete artifacts (code for E001–E004, design specs for E005, copy deliverable for E006, documentation for E007) — no type is misclassified. Both strategy open questions were correctly dispositioned as non-gating rather than Investigations: the Visibility API RBAC question does not change E002's implementation (403 is handled gracefully either way; the question is captured as a validation AC with a documented finding), and the indicator-form question is handled via placeholder treatment finalized in E005. Neither answer changes which epics exist or what they do, so no Investigation epic is warranted. No `gated_by` or `gate_failure_impact` fields are set anywhere, which is consistent with the absence of gates.\n44\t\n45\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (1/2)\n46\t\n47\tAll epics are Implementation type and all carry the 9-signal `ai_signals` field with `investigation_signals: null` — correct field type throughout. Signal rationale files exist for all seven epics, and every rationale matches its frontmatter value. Most values are consistent with strategy content: `human_process_gates: -1` on the review-gated deliverables (E005 design sign-off, E006 content review, E007 docs process) and 0/+1 elsewhere; `change_specificity: 0` for the open-scope design and docs deliverables; `open_questions` values correctly track which epics retain residual strategy questions (E002, E003) versus fully specified ones (E001, E004). One arguable value: E006 scores `repo_access: +1` (\"wording lands as UI strings in the publicly clonable odh-dashboard repository\") while the comparable non-code deliverables E005 and E007 both score 0 — a content-authoring epic is not repo-based work in the same sense. The value is defensible (the copy must be keyed to the in-repo override lookup structure) but inconsistent with its sibling deliverable epics.\n48\t\n49\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n50\t\n51\tEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria, and all 11 strategy acceptance criteria are carried into epic ACs nearly verbatim, each with its measure. Rule-mandated ACs are present where applicable: E007 (docs-authoring) requires technical review against the implemented behavior and architecture context, not just strategy text; E001's replacement of the event-driven status model is covered by the purely-additive/conditional backwards-compatibility AC; no konflux chain applies to this decomposition. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID — E002 and E006 refer to \"the state-driven workload status model\" by name rather than \"E001\". E005/E006 deliverable ACs (\"delivered with decision recorded\", \"covers every known condition/reason combination\") are testable as deliverable-existence checks.\n52\t\n53\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n54\t\n55\tAll strategy scope is accounted for: 11/11 HLRs, 11/11 acceptance criteria, and the NFRs distributed as epic ACs (unknown-condition raw-message fallback and additive conditionality on E001; 403 graceful degradation on E002; conditional display on E003/E004). All three risks are carried into the relevant epics (RBAC validation on E002, content-quality risk on E006, GitOps bypass risk on E003), and both open questions are dispositioned with traceability. The Staff Engineer overrides are respected: no rhods-operator work, and the AI-generated \"Proposed Solution\" extras (Resources column, quota preview, estimated wait times, historical data, queue details panel) are correctly excluded per the definitive HLR list and out-of-scope table — the \"scope trap\" is avoided and documented. Documentation Support is Required → E007 `docs-authoring` epic is present (Rule 11 satisfied). No conditional branches are claimed, and none are needed (no bounded-outcome decisions gate structure). Minor (not scored): the success criterion \"Scalability: handles large numbers of queued workloads\" is not an explicit AC on any epic; it is only partially covered by E002's UI-responsiveness performance AC.\n56\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a893d0885dac992a","agent_id":"a89fe66b116f94c03","session_id":"77a263a2-290e-4bf2-aaf1-488c79b76973","uuid":"7fbbe984-f4ac-4dfc-af0d-571d6230c1cc","timestamp":"2026-10-22T10:37:46.429Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8dd772eb105a56f8","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8002 decomposition"}
