{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b9235e9a60eec374","type":"tool_result","content":"RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md:16:  open_questions: 0\nRHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md:18:  human_process_gates: 1\nRHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md:15:  open_questions: 0\nRHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md:17:  human_process_gates: 0\nRHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md:15:  open_questions: 1\nRHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md:17:  human_process_gates: 0\nRHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md:15:  open_questions: 1\nRHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md:17:  human_process_gates: 1\nRHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md:16:  open_questions: 1\nRHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md:18:  human_process_gates: -1\nRHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md:22:  open_questions: 0\nRHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md:24:  human_process_gates: -1\nRHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md:15:  open_questions: 0\nRHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md:17:  human_process_gates: -1\n--- draft ID refs in bodies ---\n--- occurrences of E00x tokens after frontmatter ---\n1:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 29: # State-driven Kueue workload status model and status display\n2:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 30: \n3:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 31: ## Description\n4:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 32: \n5:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 33: Rebuilds the workbench scheduling status display in the odh-dashboard frontend so that workload scheduling status is derived state-driven from Workload CR conditions and pod/container statuses, replacing the current event-driven model that matches hardcoded progress steps with wildcard/substring event matching. The status model distinguishes preemption from other eviction reasons, surfaces requeued/retry state, and guarantees that a human-readable message is always shown: a friendly override for known condition/reason combinations, with the raw Kueue condition message as an unconditional fallback. Under no circumstance does the UI show no information when a Kueue condition exists.\n6:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 34: \n7:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 35: ## Scope\n8:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 36: \n9:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 37: - Extend the `WorkloadCondition` type union in `k8sTypes.ts`: remove the incorrect `'Failed'` entry (failure is expressed as `Finished` with reason `Failed`); include `QuotaReserved`, `Admitted`, `PodsReady`, `Finished`, `Evicted`, `Preempted`, `Requeued`, `BlockedOnPreemptionGates`, `DeactivationTarget`.\n10:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 38: - Replace wildcard/substring matching of events with a structured, extensible status derivation model driven by Workload CR conditions and pod/container statuses.\n11:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 39: - Distinguish \"Preempted\" (higher-priority workload took resources) from \"Evicted\" (admin action or timeout). Eviction reasons to handle include: Preempted, PodsReadyTimeout, AdmissionCheck, ClusterQueueStopped, LocalQueueStopped, Deactivated, NodeFailures, MaximumExecutionTimeExceeded.\n12:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 40: - Surface the Requeued state with retry count and next retry time computed from `requeueState` (including `requeueAt`) when a workload is evicted and waiting to re-enter the queue.\n13:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 41: - Provide human-friendly message overrides for known condition/reason combinations as a lookup table, with reasonable engineering-default copy; the fallback is always the raw Kueue condition `.message` field. Default copy must be structured for drop-in replacement when the finalized content deliverable arrives.\n14:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 42: - Improve status message granularity: distinguish WaitForRecovery from WaitForStart, and surface AdmissionCheck pending/retry states.\n15:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 43: - Unknown condition types or reasons must be shown with raw message text, never silently dropped.\n16:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 44: \n17:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 45: ## Acceptance Criteria\n18:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 46: \n19:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 47: - Given a Kueue-managed namespace where a workbench has been evicted due to `ClusterQueueStopped`, when the user views the workbench status, then the status displays \"Evicted\" with reason \"Queue stopped by administrator\" — NOT \"Preempted\", measured by the status label text and variant differing from the Preempted state.\n20:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 48: - Given a workbench has been evicted and the Workload CR has a `Requeued` condition with `requeueState.requeueAt` set, when the user views the workbench status, then the status displays the retry information including next retry time and attempt count, measured by the status text including the computed retry time from `requeueState`.\n21:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 49: - Given a workbench's Workload CR has a condition type or reason not recognized by the dashboard's status model, when the workbench status is rendered, then the raw Kueue condition message is displayed rather than a blank state, measured by the status text containing the Workload CR's condition `.message` field content.\n22:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 50: - Given a Workload CR contains condition types not yet in the dashboard's `WorkloadCondition` type union, when status is rendered, then the condition is shown with raw message text and never silently dropped (backwards compatibility).\n23:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 51: - All changes are purely additive and conditional: existing workbench behavior is unchanged when Kueue is not enabled or the namespace is not Kueue-managed.\n24:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 52: - The override lookup table uses placeholder default copy structured so the finalized wording can replace it without rework of the derivation logic.\n25:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 53: \n26:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 54: ## HLR Traceability\n27:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 55: \n28:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 56: - [P0] Distinguish Preempted from other Eviction reasons (ClusterQueueStopped, LocalQueueStopped, PodsReadyTimeout, Deactivated, etc.) in the workbench status display\n29:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 57: - [P0] Surface Requeued state with retry count and next retry time from `requeueState` when a workload is evicted and waiting to re-enter the queue\n30:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 58: - [P0] Display workload scheduling status derived from Workload CR conditions with human-friendly message overrides and raw message fallback\n31:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 59: - [P2] Improve status model progress steps to ensure all information related to workload scheduling is surfaced — move away from wildcard/substring matching of events to a structured, extensible status derivation model\n32:RHAISTRAT-8002-E001.md: 60: - [P2] Improve status message granularity: distinguish WaitForRecovery from WaitForStart, surface AdmissionCheck pending/retry states\n1:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 30: # Kueue Visibility API integration for queue position display\n2:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 31: \n3:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 32: ## Description\n4:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 33: \n5:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 34: Integrates Kueue's Visibility API (aggregated API server, API group `visibility.kueue.x-k8s.io`) into the odh-dashboard workbenches overview so that queue position is displayed for pending workloads. Queue position is not a field on the Workload object — it lives on the `PendingWorkload` type served via the Visibility API's `PendingWorkloadsSummary` subresource on LocalQueues. All Kubernetes API calls use the logged-in user's token through the existing `/api/k8s` passthrough proxy and are subject to the user's RBAC. Position info is supplementary: a 403 on the Visibility API must fail silently.\n6:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 35: \n7:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 36: This work depends on the state-driven workload status model (which determines pending state and provides the scheduling-status display surface the position renders into); it builds on that model rather than replicating it.\n8:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 37: \n9:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 38: ## Scope\n10:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 39: \n11:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 40: - Fetch queue position data via the LocalQueue pendingworkloads subresource: `GET /apis/visibility.kueue.x-k8s.io/v1beta2/namespaces/{ns}/localqueues/{name}/pendingworkloads`, using the logged-in user's token via the `/api/k8s` passthrough proxy.\n12:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 41: - Display `positionInLocalQueue` (and `positionInClusterQueue` where available to the user) for workloads in QueuingPending state in the workbenches overview.\n13:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 42: - Graceful degradation on 403 using the existing `getGenericErrorCode` pattern from `DistributedWorkloadsContext.tsx`: position info is silently omitted, no error is displayed.\n14:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 43: - Validate whether the `pending-workloads-lq-viewer-role` ClusterRole (aggregated to `batch-user`, `view`, `admin`, `edit`) is effectively granted to data scientists in RHOAI clusters by default, and document the finding. The implementation handles 403 correctly either way; the finding determines whether position display is broadly available or effectively admin-only, and informs position UI polish investment.\n15:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 44: \n16:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 45: ## Acceptance Criteria\n17:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 46: \n18:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 47: - Given a workbench is in QueuingPending state and the user's token has `batch-user` RBAC to the Visibility API, when the workbenches overview page renders, then the queue position is displayed (position in local queue), measured by the position number being present and matching the value from the `pendingworkloads` subresource.\n19:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 48: - Given a workbench is in QueuingPending state and the user's token does NOT have RBAC to the Visibility API, when the workbenches overview page renders, then the queue position is silently omitted with no error displayed, measured by the absence of both position data and error indicators.\n20:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 49: - The RBAC availability validation finding is documented; behavior is correct (graceful degradation) regardless of the outcome.\n21:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 50: - UI remains responsive with the Kueue Visibility API integration (performance success criterion).\n22:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 51: - The workbenches overview handles large numbers of queued workloads (scalability success criterion): given a Kueue-managed namespace with a large volume of pending workloads (100+ entries returned across the namespace's LocalQueue `pendingworkloads` subresources), the overview renders queue position and scheduling status for every pending workbench with no dropped, blank, or stale status entries, measured by all pending workbench rows displaying position/status data consistent with the subresource values under that volume.\n23:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 52: - Only factual queue position data is displayed — no estimated wait time predictions (Kueue does not provide them).\n24:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 53: \n25:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 54: ## HLR Traceability\n26:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 55: \n27:RHAISTRAT-8002-E002.md: 56: - [P1] Integrate Kueue Visibility API for queue position display (`positionInLocalQueue`, `positionInClusterQueue`) via `PendingWorkloadsSummary` subresource on LocalQueue — gracefully degrade on 403\n1:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 29: # Kueue context indicators on project details and workbenches overview\n2:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 30: \n3:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 31: ## Description\n4:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 32: \n5:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 33: Adds two conditional UI indicators to the odh-dashboard that communicate Kueue management context to users. First, a positive indicator on the project details page when the namespace is Kueue-managed, answering the question a user will inevitably ask when their workload does not start immediately. Second, an anomaly indicator on workbenches in Kueue-managed namespaces that are NOT managed by Kueue (missing the queue label) — serving the hybrid GitOps + dashboard user. Steady-state workloads that ARE Kueue-managed get no redundant \"managed by Kueue\" badge. Visual treatment ships with a placeholder that is replaced when the finalized UX design (RHOAIUX-7470) is delivered; engineering is not blocked on that design.\n6:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 34: \n7:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 35: ## Scope\n8:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 36: \n9:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 37: - Project details page: positive indicator (info banner, badge, or label — exact form finalized by UX under RHOAIUX-7470; placeholder treatment during development) shown when the namespace has the `kueue.openshift.io/managed=true` label, stating that the project uses queue-based scheduling.\n10:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 38: - Workbenches overview: anomaly indicator shown ONLY on workbenches in a Kueue-managed namespace that are missing the `kueue.x-k8s.io/queue-name` label (created via GitOps or CLI bypassing the dashboard). Workload-to-Notebook matching uses the existing `buildWorkloadMapForNotebooks()` logic in `api/k8s/workloads.ts`.\n11:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 39: - The dashboard warns about missing queue labels but never blocks workload creation because of them.\n12:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 40: - Conditional display: all indicators appear only when the namespace is Kueue-managed; existing workbench behavior is unchanged otherwise (purely additive).\n13:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 41: \n14:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 42: ## Acceptance Criteria\n15:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 43: \n16:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 44: - Given a Kueue-managed namespace, when the user views the project details page, then a positive indicator is visible stating this project uses queue-based scheduling, measured by the indicator element being present in the DOM when the namespace label `kueue.openshift.io/managed=true` exists and absent when it does not.\n17:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 45: - Given a Kueue-managed namespace containing a workbench that does NOT have the `kueue.x-k8s.io/queue-name` label (created via GitOps or CLI without queue assignment), when the user views the workbenches overview, then that specific workbench displays a visual indicator that it is not managed by Kueue, measured by the anomaly indicator being present only on non-Kueue workloads and absent on Kueue-managed workloads.\n18:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 46: - The placeholder visual treatment is structured so the finalized UX design can replace it without rework of the conditional logic.\n19:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 47: - Existing workbench behavior is unchanged when the namespace is not Kueue-managed.\n20:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 48: \n21:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 49: ## HLR Traceability\n22:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 50: \n23:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 51: - [P0] Add positive Kueue indicator on project details page when namespace has `kueue.openshift.io/managed=true` label\n24:RHAISTRAT-8002-E003.md: 52: - [P0] Add anomaly indicator on workloads in a Kueue-managed namespace that are NOT managed by Kueue (missing `kueue.x-k8s.io/queue-name` label)\n1:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 29: # HardwareProfile form and popover Kueue enhancements\n2:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 30: \n3:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 31: ## Description\n4:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 32: \n5:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 33: Hardens the HardwareProfile-related UI in the odh-dashboard for Kueue-managed contexts. The HP details popover correctly handles GitOps-managed notebooks whose queue label was applied directly (no matching HardwareProfile), the HP admin form validates LocalQueue names, the filtered HP dropdown in the workbench creation form explains why only queue-enabled profiles are shown, and workbench creation warns (but does not block) when a referenced LocalQueue does not exist in the target namespace.\n6:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 34: \n7:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 35: ## Scope\n8:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 36: \n9:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 37: - HardwareProfile details popover: when no HP scheduling data exists for a notebook, fall back to reading the `kueue.x-k8s.io/queue-name` label from the notebook's own labels (a supported GitOps pattern). Resolve the cluster queue name from the project's LocalQueue list. Indicate the source: \"Queue: my-queue (applied directly)\" vs. \"Queue: my-queue (via hardware profile)\".\n10:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 38: - HardwareProfile admin form: validate the LocalQueue name field against `k8sNameRegex` (`/^[a-z0-9]([-a-z0-9]*[a-z0-9])?$/`). Existence cannot be validated because HardwareProfiles are cluster-scoped while LocalQueues are namespace-scoped (target namespace unknown at HP creation time).\n11:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 39: - Workbench creation form: if the specified LocalQueue does not exist in the target namespace, display a dismissible warning — NOT an error, and never blocking creation. The admin may have intended the LQ to be created later or delivered via GitOps; Kueue itself reports the error via the Workload's conditions if the queue truly does not exist.\n12:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 40: - HardwareProfile dropdown in the workbench creation form (Kueue-managed namespaces): keep strict filtering to queue-enabled profiles only, and add an info icon or tooltip explaining \"Only queue-enabled hardware profiles are shown because this project uses queue-based scheduling.\" The principle: be opinionated, but tell users we are being opinionated.\n13:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 41: \n14:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 42: ## Acceptance Criteria\n15:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 43: \n16:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 44: - Given a GitOps-managed workbench with `kueue.x-k8s.io/queue-name` label applied directly (no matching HardwareProfile), when the user opens the HardwareProfile details popover, then the popover displays the queue name with indication it was applied directly (not via hardware profile), measured by the popover text including the queue name and a source indicator.\n17:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 45: - Given an admin is creating a HardwareProfile and enters an invalid LocalQueue name (e.g., contains uppercase or spaces), when the form validates, then a validation error is shown for the LocalQueue name field, measured by the form displaying an inline error referencing the Kubernetes name format.\n18:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 46: - Given a workbench is being created with a HardwareProfile referencing a LocalQueue that does not exist in the target namespace, when the creation form is submitted, then a warning (not error) is displayed indicating the local queue was not found, measured by the warning being dismissible and not blocking workbench creation.\n19:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 47: - Given a workbench is in a Kueue-managed namespace and the user opens the workbench creation form, when the HardwareProfile dropdown renders with only queue-enabled profiles, then an info indicator explains why only queue-enabled profiles are shown, measured by the presence of a tooltip or info icon adjacent to the dropdown.\n20:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 48: - All changes are conditional on Kueue-managed context; behavior in non-Kueue namespaces is unchanged.\n21:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 49: \n22:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 50: ## HLR Traceability\n23:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 51: \n24:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 52: - [P1] Fix HardwareProfile details popover for GitOps-managed notebooks: read queue label directly when no HP match exists, indicate source\n25:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 53: - [P1] Add `k8sNameRegex` validation to LocalQueue name field in HardwareProfile admin form\n26:RHAISTRAT-8002-E004.md: 54: - [P1] Add explicit info indicator on HP dropdown explaining why only queue-enabled profiles are shown in Kueue-managed namespaces\n1:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 29: # UX visual treatment for Kueue indicators and status messaging (RHOAIUX-7470)\n2:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 30: \n3:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 31: ## Description\n4:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 32: \n5:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 33: UXD deliverable tracked under RHOAIUX-7470: finalized visual treatments for the Kueue-related UI additions in the workbenches experience — the positive Kueue indicator on the project details page, the anomaly indicator for non-Kueue workloads in Kueue-managed namespaces, the HardwareProfile dropdown info indicator, and content review of the status message overrides. Engineering proceeds in parallel with placeholder treatments and is explicitly not blocked on this deliverable; the finalized designs replace the placeholders when delivered.\n6:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 34: \n7:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 35: ## Scope\n8:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 36: \n9:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 37: - Decide the form of the positive Kueue indicator on the project details page (info banner vs. badge vs. label) and deliver the finalized visual treatment. The requirement being designed for is non-negotiable: users must know their project uses queue-based scheduling because it fundamentally affects how their workloads start.\n10:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 38: - Design the anomaly indicator for workloads in Kueue-managed namespaces that are not Kueue-managed (missing queue label) — distinct from steady-state \"managed by Kueue\" affordances, which are deliberately absent.\n11:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 39: - Design the info indicator / tooltip treatment for the HardwareProfile dropdown that explains why only queue-enabled profiles are shown in Kueue-managed namespaces (\"be opinionated, but tell users we are being opinionated\").\n12:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 40: - Collaborate on content review of the human-friendly status message overrides for Kueue conditions (joint UXD/Content review under RHOAIUX-7470).\n13:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 41: - Deliver design specs structured so they can replace engineering placeholder treatments without rework of the conditional logic.\n14:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 42: \n15:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 43: ## Acceptance Criteria\n16:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 44: \n17:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 45: - The finalized visual treatment for the positive Kueue indicator is delivered, with the banner/badge/label decision recorded and rationale.\n18:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 46: - Visual designs for the workload anomaly indicator and the HP dropdown info indicator are delivered.\n19:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 47: - Status message content review (joint with the Content team under RHOAIUX-7470) is completed for the override wording.\n20:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 48: - Deliverables are specified against the implemented conditional behaviors so placeholder treatments can be swapped with minimal engineering rework.\n21:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 49: \n22:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 50: ## HLR Traceability\n23:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 51: \n24:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 52: - [P0] Add positive Kueue indicator on project details page when namespace has `kueue.openshift.io/managed=true` label (finalized visual treatment)\n25:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 53: - [P0] Add anomaly indicator on workloads in a Kueue-managed namespace that are NOT managed by Kueue (finalized visual treatment)\n26:RHAISTRAT-8002-E005.md: 54: - [P1] Add explicit info indicator on HP dropdown explaining why only queue-enabled profiles are shown in Kueue-managed namespaces (finalized visual treatment)\n1:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 30: # Human-friendly status message microcopy for Kueue conditions (RHOAIUX-7470)\n2:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 31: \n3:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 32: ## Description\n4:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 33: \n5:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 34: Content-team deliverable tracked under RHOAIUX-7470: final human-readable wording for the status message override table that maps known Kueue condition/reason combinations to friendly messages in the workbench status display. Raw Kueue condition messages are unintelligible to data scientists; this deliverable replaces the engineering default copy. Engineering is explicitly not blocked on it — reasonable defaults ship first and are replaced when content review is complete.\n6:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 35: \n7:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 36: This work builds on the state-driven workload status model, which defines the override lookup structure and the enumerated set of known condition/reason combinations the wording must cover.\n8:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 37: \n9:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 38: ## Scope\n10:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 39: \n11:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 40: - Author human-friendly message overrides for all known Kueue condition/reason combinations recognized by the status model, including: eviction reasons (Preempted, PodsReadyTimeout, AdmissionCheck, ClusterQueueStopped, LocalQueueStopped, Deactivated, NodeFailures, MaximumExecutionTimeExceeded), Requeued/retry states, AdmissionCheck pending/retry states, and the WaitForRecovery vs. WaitForStart distinction.\n12:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 41: - Ensure wording targets non-Kubernetes users (data scientists) while remaining accurate to the underlying condition semantics.\n13:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 42: - Complete content review with UXD under RHOAIUX-7470.\n14:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 43: - Deliver the wording keyed to the override lookup structure so it drops in without changes to the derivation logic or the raw-message fallback.\n15:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 44: \n16:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 45: ## Acceptance Criteria\n17:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 46: \n18:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 47: - Override copy covers every known condition/reason combination recognized by the status model's override table.\n19:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 48: - Wording is reviewed and approved via the RHOAIUX-7470 content review process.\n20:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 49: - Unknown condition/reason combinations continue to fall back to the raw Kueue condition `.message` text — the copy deliverable never introduces a blank state.\n21:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 50: - The delivered wording replaces the engineering defaults without rework of the status derivation logic.\n22:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 51: \n23:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 52: ## HLR Traceability\n24:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 53: \n25:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 54: - [P0] Display workload scheduling status derived from Workload CR conditions with human-friendly message overrides and raw message fallback (final override wording)\n26:RHAISTRAT-8002-E006.md: 55: - [P2] Improve status message granularity: distinguish WaitForRecovery from WaitForStart, surface AdmissionCheck pending/retry states (final wording)\n1:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 35: # Documentation: release note and feature documentation determination\n2:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 36: \n3:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 37: ## Description\n4:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 38: \n5:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 39: Documentation deliverable for the Kueue workload scheduling visibility features in the workbenches overview. At minimum a release note is required; the epic also determines whether feature-level documentation is warranted and authors it if so. Documentation runs last: it describes what was built, after all implementation, UX, and content work concludes.\n6:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 40: \n7:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 41: ## Scope\n8:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 42: \n9:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 43: - Author a release note covering the new workload scheduling status display (eviction-reason distinction, requeued/retry information), the project-level positive Kueue indicator, the workload anomaly indicator, queue position display (including its RBAC-dependent availability), and the HardwareProfile form/popover changes (LocalQueue name validation, GitOps queue-label fallback, dropdown info indicator, non-blocking LocalQueue-not-found warning).\n10:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 44: - Determine whether feature-level documentation is warranted for the Kueue scheduling visibility capabilities and record the decision with rationale; author feature documentation if warranted.\n11:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 45: - Document the user-facing behavior boundaries: indicators and status information appear only in Kueue-managed namespaces; queue position may be absent for users without Visibility API RBAC; estimated wait times are not provided.\n12:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 46: \n13:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 47: ## Acceptance Criteria\n14:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 48: \n15:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 49: - A release note covering all user-visible changes in this strategy is published.\n16:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 50: - The decision on feature-level documentation (warranted or not) is recorded with rationale; if warranted, the feature documentation is authored.\n17:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 51: - All documentation is technically reviewed against the implemented behavior — accuracy validated against the actual implementation and the platform architecture context, not just the strategy text.\n18:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 52: - Documentation correctly states the conditional nature of the features (Kueue-managed namespaces only) and the graceful-degradation behaviors users may observe.\n19:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 53: \n20:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 54: ## HLR Traceability\n21:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 55: \n22:RHAISTRAT-8002-E007.md: 56: - All high level requirements of this strategy at the documentation level: P0 eviction-reason distinction, P0 requeued state, P0 positive Kueue indicator, P0 anomaly indicator, P0 status display with overrides, P1 Visibility API queue position, P1 HP popover fallback, P1 LocalQueue name validation, P1 HP dropdown info indicator, P2 structured status model, P2 status message granularity.\ndone","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b7a3dd601a276d44","agent_id":"a2fe83627be0e5be3","session_id":"77a263a2-290e-4bf2-aaf1-488c79b76973","uuid":"c16bf0a9-05a1-49e0-94e4-d125b9f77fd7","timestamp":"2026-10-22T10:43:49.238Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b9235e9a60eec374","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8002 decomposition"}
